1. FOUR NUMBERS PICK A SET
   A toy system, small enough to print whole:
     radix beta = 2    precision p = 3 digits    exponent e from -1 to 2
   A member is  d0.d1d2 (in binary) x 2^e,  with d0 = 1:

     significand     e = -1    e = 0    e = 1    e = 2
       1.00 = 1         0.5        1        2        4
       1.01 = 1.25    0.625     1.25      2.5        5
       1.10 = 1.5      0.75      1.5        3        6
       1.11 = 1.75    0.875     1.75      3.5        7

   16 positive, 16 negative and zero: 33 members.
   Per sign: (beta - 1) x beta^(p-1) x (number of exponents) = 1 x 4 x 4 = 16.
   Largest 7, smallest positive 0.5.
   Every other real number -- 0.1, 1/3, 0.51, pi -- is simply not in the set.

2. THE GAPS GROW WITH THE NUMBERS
   Every multiple of 1/8 from 0 to 7, marked where a member sits:

     0       1       2       3       4       5       6       7
     |...|||||.|.|.|.|...|...|...|...|.......|.......|.......|

   Between one power of 2 and the next, the members are evenly spaced:
     from 0.5 to 1     gap 0.125
     from   1 to 2     gap 0.25
     from   2 to 4     gap 0.5
     from   4 to 7     gap 1
   The gap doubles when the number doubles, so the gap RELATIVE to the
   number stays between 1/7 and 1/4 everywhere. A float keeps a fixed
   number of significant digits, never a fixed number of decimal places.

   THE HOLE AT ZERO. Below 0.5 there is nothing but 0. So two different
   members can have a difference too small to be a member:
     0.625 (-) 0.5  =  round(0.125)  =  0      although 0.625 != 0.5
   IEEE 754 fills the hole with SUBNORMALS: d0 = 0 is allowed at e = -1.

     |||||||||.|.|.|.|...|...|...|...|.......|.......|.......|

     0.625 (-) 0.5  =  round(0.125)  =  0.125
   From here on the toy system has them: 19 per sign, 39 members.

3. ROUNDING IS A FUNCTION, AND IEEE 754 DEFINES FIVE
   Each one sends every real number to a member, or to an infinity:
     RD toward -inf    RU toward +inf    RZ toward zero
     RN to nearest, a tie going to the EVEN significand or AWAY from zero

     x          RD        RU        RZ        RN_even   RN_away
     1/10       0         0.125     0         0.125     0.125
     1/3        0.25      0.375     0.25      0.375     0.375
     1.625      1.5       1.75      1.5       1.5       1.75
     -1.625     -1.75     -1.5      -1.5      -1.5      -1.75
     7.25       7         +inf      7         7         7
     7.5        7         +inf      7         +inf      +inf
     0.03125    0         0.125     0         0         0

   1.625 is a tie: 1.5 = 1.10 ends in 0 and 1.75 = 1.11 does not.
   7.5 is half a gap past the largest member, so both RNs overflow.

   Checked on every multiple of 1/64 from -8 to 8 (1,025 values):
     RD(x) <= x <= RU(x)                                True
     both RNs always return RD(x) or RU(x)              True
     RZ(x) is RD(x) when x >= 0, and RU(x) when x < 0   True

   Python's float only ever uses RN even. Its decimal module has all five:
     x      ROUND_FLOOR     ROUND_CEILING   ROUND_DOWN      ROUND_HALF_EVEN ROUND_HALF_UP
     2.5    2               3               2               2               3
     -2.5   -3              -2              -2              -2              -3
     2.4    2               3               2               2               2
   (ROUND_HALF_UP is the decimal module's name for ties away from zero.)

   An operation is the exact result, then a rounding function:
     a (+) b = RN(a + b),  and the same for (-), (x) and (/).
     1.25 (+) 0.625 = RN(1.875) = 2      0.125 (x) 0.25 = RN(0.03125) = 0
   From here on, every operation rounds to nearest, ties to even.

4. WHICH LAWS OF ARITHMETIC SURVIVE
   Tested on every member (39), every pair (1,521) and every triple
   (59,319) of the toy system. Cases where a step overflows are skipped.

   a (+) b  =  b (+) a
       holds: 0 failures in 1,401 pairs
   a (x) b  =  b (x) a
       holds: 0 failures in 1,233 pairs
   a <= b   implies   a (+) c <= b (+) c
       holds: 0 failures in 26,162 triples
   a (-) b = 0   implies   a = b
       holds: 0 failures in 1,401 pairs
       ...but without subnormals: 44 failures in 969 pairs, e.g. 0.625 (-) 0.5 = 0
   b/2 <= a <= 2b   implies   a (-) b is exact  (Sterbenz)
       holds: 0 failures in 139 pairs
       ...but without subnormals: 28 failures in 124 pairs

   (a (+) b) (+) c  =  a (+) (b (+) c)
       FAILS: 13,972 failures in 47,823 triples
       (1 (+) 0.125) (+) 0.125 = 1,  but  1 (+) (0.125 (+) 0.125) = 1.25
   a (x) (b (+) c)  =  (a (x) b) (+) (a (x) c)
       FAILS: 9,444 failures in 37,463 triples
       1.5 (x) (1 (+) 0.125) = 1.5,  but  (1.5 (x) 1) (+) (1.5 (x) 0.125) = 1.75
   b != 0   implies   a (+) b != a
       FAILS: 80 failures in 1,362 pairs
       4 (+) 0.125 = 4
   a (x) (1 (/) a)  =  1
       FAILS: 8 failures in 36 members
       7 (x) (1 (/) 7) = 7 (x) 0.125 = 0.875

   The survivors each compare one exact result with itself: a + b and
   b + a are the same real number before any rounding happens. The
   failures compare two ROUTES, and each route rounds in different places.

5. THE FLOAT YOU ALREADY USE IS THE SAME CONSTRUCTION
   Read off this machine:  beta = 2,  p = 53,  e from -1022 to 1023
     members              18,437,736,874,454,810,623
       equal to 2^64 - 2^53 - 1?      True
     largest              1.7976931348623157e+308
       equal to (2 - 2^-52) x 2^1023? True
     smallest normal      2.2250738585072014e-308  = 2^-1022: True
     smallest subnormal   5e-324  = 2^-1074: True
     gap above 1.0        2.220446049250313e-16
     gap above 1e+16      2.0
     gap above 1e+308     1.99584030953472e+292

   The laws of section 4, on real floats:
     0.1 + 0.2 == 0.2 + 0.1       True
     (0.1 + 0.2) + 0.3            0.6000000000000001
     0.1 + (0.2 + 0.3)            0.6
     100.0 * (0.1 + 0.2)          30.000000000000004
     100.0 * 0.1 + 100.0 * 0.2    30.0
     2.0**53 + 1.0 == 2.0**53     True
     49.0 * (1.0 / 49.0)          0.9999999999999999
     (0.1 + 0.2) - 0.3            5.551115123125783e-17
       ...exactly the difference of the two stored values? True

   Which fractions can be members at ANY precision? In radix beta, a
   fraction in lowest terms can be, only if every prime factor of its
   denominator also divides beta:

                radix 2   radix 10  radix 3
     1/10       no        yes       no
     1/3        no        no        yes
     3/8        yes       yes       no
     1/6        no        no        no

6. OFF THE ENDS OF THE RANGE
   The largest float is about 1.8e308. Factorials outrun it quickly:
     170!  as a float  = 7.257415615307994e+306
     171!  as a float  = inf
     1000! has 2,568 digits, 1001! has 2,571

   1001! / 1000! is exactly 1001. With floats:
     1001! -> inf    1000! -> inf    inf / inf = nan
   If overflow stopped at the largest float instead of going to inf:
     largest / largest = 1.0     a quiet, plausible, wrong answer

   Three ways to stay inside the set:
     cancel first      1001! / 1000!  =  1001 x 1000! / 1000!  =  1001
     exact integers    factorial(1001) // factorial(1000)  =  1001
     logarithms        exp(lgamma(1002) - lgamma(1001))  =  1001   to 9 s.f.

   And off the bottom, where the smallest subnormal is 5e-324:
     5e-324 / 2  =  0.0     a tie between 0 and 5e-324, and 0 is even

7. CHOOSING THE FOUR NUMBERS IS THE COMPROMISE
                bits  radix    p    emin   emax   largest     members
     binary16     16      2   11     -14     15   6.55E+4     6.35e+04
     binary32     32      2   24    -126    127   3.40E+38    4.28e+09
     binary64     64      2   53   -1022   1023   1.79E+308   1.84e+19
     decimal64    64     10   16    -383    384   9.99E+384   1.38e+19
   Brute force on binary16: its 65,536 bit patterns give 63,487 distinct
   finite values. The count formula gives 63,487.

   What each one stores for a few numbers, as a relative error:
                1/10        1/3         2^24 + 1    70,000
     binary16   -2.4e-04    -2.4e-04    overflow    overflow
     binary32   +1.5e-08    +3.0e-08    -6.0e-08    exact
     binary64   +5.6e-17    -5.6e-17    exact       exact
     decimal64  exact       -1.0e-16    exact       exact

   Relative gap just above 1, and just below the next power of the radix:
     binary64    2.2e-16  and  1.1e-16    varies 2x
     decimal64   1.0e-15  and  1.0e-16    varies 10x

   More bits buy precision and range, and cost memory and speed. Radix 10
   makes 1/10 exact, but its gaps wobble 10x instead of 2x, and the same
   64 bits give fewer distinct values. Every choice gives something up.
